
## Part 2

1. Describe a person who solve problem in a smart way
   One person who helped me solve a problem in a **genuinely** smart way is my manager at work. I’m studying computer science, and **at one point** my coursework **collided** with a major work deadline. **The pressure was mounting**, and I wasn’t just busy - my head was completely **cluttered**. I kept **task-switching**, **overanalysing everything**, and my **mental bandwidth was basically gone**.
  He **picked up on it** immediately and had a **frank**, **no-nonsense** chat with me. The first thing he taught me was **ruthless prioritisation**. He made me do a quick **brain-dump** of every commitment **on my plate**, and then he asked, “Which two or three things actually **move the needle**?” **Cutting** everything else **down** to **“nice-to-have”** instantly reduced the noise and **gave me a clear line of sight**.
  Next, he **pushed me to** **time-block** my day. Instead of vague plans, I set **non-negotiable** blocks for deep work and treated them like meetings I couldn’t cancel. His message was simple: don’t **spiral into** planning - **bias toward action**. Just start, **build momentum**, and let progress calm your mind.
  The most memorable part was his final technique: he told me to run a quick **worst-case-scenario check** - almost like a mini **pre-mortem**. If the worst case was tolerable, I needed to stop **catastrophising** and **dial down the stress**. If the consequences were serious, that was my signal to tackle it early rather than let it **snowball**.
  I think it’s brilliant because it’s not a motivational speech - it’s a **repeatable framework**. It helped me **regain control**, protect my focus, and **stay composed under pressure**. I still use it whenever I feel overwhelmed.
